-- title:
--   Injectivity of the divisorial Weil pairing, n invertible
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field which is simultaneously a $K$-algebra and an algebra over the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$, compatibly (scalar tower), and which is finite-dimensional over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$; assume $F$ satisfies `HasPrincipalDivisors K F`, i.e. every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor, a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on the places of $F/K$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing $K$, distinct from $F$, whose valuation ring is a principal ideal ring) whose value at each $v$ is $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)$, and this divisor has degree zero. Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number with $n \neq 0$ in $K$. Assume further (`hdiv`) that for every field $L'$ that is a $K$-algebra and a $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$-algebra compatibly and finite over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$, and every integer $m$ with $m \neq 0$ in $K$, the group $\mathrm{Pic}^0(L'/K)$ — degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones — is divisible by $m$. Let $e$ be a `DivisorialWeilPairingData K F n`: a map $\mathrm{pair}$ from pairs of $n$-torsion classes in $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)$ to $K$, agreeing with the pairing attached to each Weil datum on the associated pair of classes, together with the moving property that every $n$-torsion class is represented by a degree-zero divisor supported at rational places (places whose residue field is generated by $K$) avoiding any prescribed finite set of places. Then the additive homomorphism $x \mapsto e.\mathrm{pair}(x,\cdot)$ from $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)[n]$ to the additive group of $K$-valued additive characters of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)[n]$ is injective.
--
--   This is the injectivity half of the non-degeneracy of the Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion of the Jacobian of the curve with function field $F$, here in the divisorial formulation and for any $n$ invertible in $K$. It is combined with a counting or duality argument in [`AlgebraicCurve.DivisorialWeilPairingData.perfect_of_divisible_coprime_of_isAlgClosed`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.DivisorialWeilPairingData.perfect_of_divisible_coprime_of_isAlgClosed) to obtain perfectness of the pairing.
